<p>Iranian state media and multiple international outlets say Iran’s Supreme Leader Ayatollah Ali Khamenei, 86, was killed in joint U.S.-Israeli air strikes that hit his compound in Tehran—an abrupt and consequential rupture at the top of the Islamic Republic. President Trump said the United States worked closely with Israel, describing the operation as a way to end a long-running threat and prevent Iran from developing a nuclear weapon. Iran announced an official mourning period and public holidays. The Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps (IRGC) vowed retaliation, and Iranian forces launched missiles and drones toward Israel and multiple Gulf states that host U.S. bases. Fighting continued into a second day, deepening fears of a wider regional escalation. Khamenei’s death also forces urgent questions about succession and governance, with reporting emphasizing how unclear it is who will ultimately emerge as the next supreme leader. Khamenei led Iran from 1989 and was widely described across outlets as a hardline ruler who centralized power, leaned on the IRGC and Basij, and oversaw repeated crackdowns on dissent while projecting Iranian influence across the Middle East. Some Iranians and members of the diaspora publicly celebrated the news of his death; others expressed grief or stayed quiet—another sign of how divided views of Iran’s political future remain.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/vevgCqg1XoaBVP8Zm_s2ibteakZ8l9S0a4Y692qGxRUPiftORXysJfdqprMrT4vbD14_uY5LvbV5QifdtfNRCaYnOZ5ZLJY1hfslBxcVEr1E' alt='Iran’s Supreme Leader Ayatollah Ali Khamenei at a Tehran commemoration in June 2025.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Family casualties: Iranian state media also said Khamenei’s daughter, grandchild, daughter-in-law and son-in-law were killed in the strikes.</li><li>Interim council: Reports said a temporary leadership council has formed, including President Masoud Pezeshkian, judiciary chief Gholamhossein Mohseni Ejei and a jurist from the Guardian Council, as the constitutional succession process begins.</li><li>Regional spillover: Reports described effects beyond Israel and Iran, including explosions near Erbil airport in Iraq’s Kurdistan region, where U.S.-led coalition forces are based, and unrest near Baghdad’s Green Zone.</li><li>Public reactions: Euronews reported mourners gathering at the Imam Reza Shrine in Mashhad, while other coverage described street celebrations in Iranian cities and among Iranian communities in North America.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Iranian Revolutionary Guard Corps (IRGC): The IRGC said it was mourning Khamenei and vowed “severe punishment” and an intensified offensive against Israel and U.S. bases. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1976870/iran-supreme-leader-ayatollah-khamenei-assassinated-in-us-israel-strikes'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>President Trump: President Trump said the U.S. worked closely with Israel, cast the strikes as preventing Iran from developing a nuclear weapon, and urged Iranians to seize the moment to take over their government. (<a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/trump-says-iranian-supreme-leader-ali-khamenei-killed-in-u-s-israeli-strikes'>PBS NewsHour</a>)</li><li>Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu: Netanyahu said there were “many signs” Khamenei was dead and described the campaign as targeting Iran’s leadership, while also calling on Iranians to oppose their rulers. (<a href='http://www.euronews.com/2026/02/28/netanyahu-on-khamenei-there-are-many-signs-that-this-dictator-is-gone'>Euronews</a>)</li><li>United Nations (António Guterres): UN Secretary-General António Guterres said he was not in a position to confirm early reports of Khamenei’s death as claims circulated. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/iran-official-warns-of-mental-warfare-after-israeli-reports-of-khameneis-death/article70689539.ece'>The Hindu</a>)</li><li>Iranian diaspora (mixed views): Iranian communities across North America held rallies both supporting and opposing the strikes, with some demonstrators voicing hope for a political change in Iran and others condemning the attack. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/02/iranians-across-north-america-rally-and-against-strikes'>Al-Monitor</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/e026b4a7-1115-493a-9b31-c8b0b5965491'>Iran’s supreme leader killed in air strikes</a> - ft.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1976870/iran-supreme-leader-ayatollah-khamenei-assassinated-in-us-israel-strikes'>Iran Supreme Leader Ayatollah Khamenei assassinated in US-Israel strikes</a> - dawn.com</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/live/2026/02/28/world/iran-strikes-trump'>Live Updates: Iran Says Supreme Leader Killed in U.S.-Israeli Strikes</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.rferl.org/a/khamenei-death-iran-us-strikes-israel-obituary/33690677.html'>Ali Khamenei, Iran’s Highest Authority, Dead At 86</a> - rferl.org</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/ali-khamenei-an-unusual-sort-of-dictator-killed-at-86/a-76169184'>Ali Khamenei: An &#x27;unusual sort of dictator&#x27; — killed at 86</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/obituaries/article/2026/03/01/ali-khamenei-iran-s-supreme-leader-and-representative-of-the-regime-s-hard-line-faction-has-died_6750973_15.html'>Ali Khamenei, Iran&#x27;s supreme leader and symbol of a  repressive regime, has died</a> - lemonde.fr</li><li><a href='https://www.economist.com/middle-east-and-africa/2026/03/01/with-the-supreme-leader-dead-power-in-iran-hangs-in-the-balance'>With the supreme leader dead, power in Iran hangs in the balance</a> - economist.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2026/mar/01/us-israel-war-on-iran-ayatollah-ali-khamenei-i-dead-latest-reports'>US-Israel war on Iran: Ayatollah Ali Khamenei confirmed dead by state media; Iran launches fresh strikes – latest reports</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.co